00:00:11.559 --> 00:00:19.019
Antarctica is earth’s coldest, most desolate,
most isolated, windiest, driest, and southernmost

00:00:19.019 --> 00:00:20.279
continent.

00:00:20.279 --> 00:00:25.189
All but 2% of the land-mass is covered in
ice thousands of feet thick.

00:00:25.189 --> 00:00:29.399
Human eyes did not gaze upon the continent
until 1820.

00:00:29.399 --> 00:00:33.580
Human feet did not touch Antarctica until
1895.

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:39.260
It is not a place built for humans but still,
thousands of people live there for up to years

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:45.910
on end, but how do they get there, how do
they live there, and how does Antarctica work?

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:50.760
Antarctica has thousands of residents, significant
infrastructure, and a large transport network

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:56.160
and yet it’s one of the very few areas of
land on earth not part of any country.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:02.210
Seven countries have made Antarctic claims—Chile,
Argentina, the United Kingdom, Norway, Australia,

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:07.009
France, and New Zealand—but they are exactly
that, claims.

00:01:07.009 --> 00:01:11.899
The only real gauge of whether a country’s
territorial claim is real is if other countries

00:01:11.899 --> 00:01:16.310
recognize it and, overwhelmingly, these claims
are not recognized.

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:20.760
Australia’s claim, for example, is only
recognized by the United Kingdom, Norway,

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:26.039
France, and New Zealand—countries which
clearly have a vested interest in the recognition

00:01:26.039 --> 00:01:27.569
of Antarctic claims.

00:01:27.569 --> 00:01:30.549
For the most part, these claims are ignored.

00:01:30.549 --> 00:01:34.539
One doesn’t go through customs upon arrival
in the claims and certain of them overlap

00:01:34.539 --> 00:01:35.899
with other claims.

00:01:35.899 --> 00:01:41.369
The more universally recognized interpretation
is that Antarctica is an international zone.

00:01:41.369 --> 00:01:45.770
Just like outer space and the ocean, Antarctica
is considered part of the common heritage

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:51.439
of mankind meaning that it should be preserved
immaculately for all future generations, forever,

00:01:51.439 --> 00:01:53.280
but that’s easier said than done.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.600
The seminal piece of legislation regulating
the continent is the Antarctic Treaty.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:03.549
Just as the cold war was heating up in the
late 1950’s, the United States, the Soviet

00:02:03.549 --> 00:02:07.939
Union, and all other countries with an interest
in Antarctica gathered together to decide

00:02:07.939 --> 00:02:09.580
how the continent would be used.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:14.020
They emerged with a future-facing treaty that
solved most political disputes and issues

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:16.590
with the continent, except for one.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:20.400
In its text, the treaty specifically says,
“Nothing contained in the present treaty

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.820
shall be interpreted as a renunciation by
any Contracting Party of previously asserted

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:28.670
rights of or claims to territorial sovereignty
in Antarctica.”

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:33.160
Essentially, they didn’t solve the sovereignty
issue because it was too difficult to solve,

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:37.600
but they did ban military presence, mining,
and nuclear explosions which has helped enormously

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:39.880
in keeping the last continent pristine.

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:42.260
So that brings us to today.

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:47.020
There are no large scale commercial operations
in Antarctica thanks to that treaty.

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:50.320
The vast majority of individuals are there
for research.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:55.220
Of course, living and maintaining a base on
the world’s most desolate continent is hugely

00:02:55.220 --> 00:03:00.600
expensive, but it’s worth it for the research
that can only be conducted in Antarctica.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:05.280
Some individuals are there to study the continent
itself—it’s wildlife, its geology, and

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:09.080
its climate—but others use the area to study
the entire world.

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:13.650
Ice cores can be used to track historic atmospheric
carbon levels, underground ponds can be tapped

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:18.190
to find ancient microbial life unique to the
area, and ice thickness can be monitored to

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:20.650
understand how sea levels will rise.

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:23.490
Scientists even use Antarctica to look at
space.

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:28.490
As such as isolated place, Antarctica has
very low background radiation and virtually

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:33.740
no light pollution which allows astronomers
to use various techniques to peer into deep

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:34.740
space.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:40.100
Scientists are performing groundbreaking research
in Antarctica, but how do they even get there?

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:44.190
The difficulty in getting to Antarctica all
stems from its weather.

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:49.540
The all-time record high at the south pole
is 9.9 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:53.320
The coasts are significantly warmer where
the average summer high is about 30 degrees

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:59.150
Fahrenheit but still, weather above freezing
anywhere in Antarctica is an anomaly.

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:04.580
As mentioned, this means that there is virtually
no bare ground—nearly the entire continent

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:07.540
is covered in thick ice and snow.

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:12.500
Therefore, the only real choice when building
an Antarctic airstrip is whether to make it

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:14.500
on ice or snow.

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:17.760
One thing to remember is that Antarctica is
a desert.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:22.250
The coastal regions, where most of the research
bases are, do experience the most snow but

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:26.220
still then, that’s a maximum of eight inches
per year.

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:30.940
The south pole, meanwhile, only sees about
2-3 inches of snowfall per year.

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:36.720
It doesn’t snow much, but when it does,
it sticks around for centuries.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:40.700
Therefore, a runway built on ice or snow is
fairly permanent.

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:44.190
It doesn’t get buried as one might in Canada
or Russia.

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:49.200
McMurdo Station’s Pegasus Field, for example,
was used for more than 40 years before it

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:53.580
closed in 2016 to be replaced by the new Phoenix
Airfield.

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:56.410
Phoenix Airfield is a compacted snow runway.

00:04:56.410 --> 00:05:01.290
Machines are used to pack the snow until it’s
dense enough to support a fully loaded, half-million

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:04.120
pound C-17 wheeled cargo plane.

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:07.520
But compacted-snow runways have a disadvantage—they
can melt.

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:11.760
During the warmest months of the summer, the
snow can warm and soften enough that it is

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:17.030
no longer safe to land wheeled aircraft so
that’s why there’s the other type of runway—blue

00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:18.490
ice runways.

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:23.620
These ice runways are built on areas of glacial
ice where’s there’s no snow accumulation.

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:29.080
Ice is much more resilient to warmer temperatures
so these runways can be used year-round.

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:33.460
Runways on the sea-ice are also used typically
at the beginning of the summer research season

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:37.610
in early November until December when the
southern hemisphere’s summer begins and

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:39.400
the ice starts to break up.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:44.330
Once the coasts are ice-free, cargo ships
can also bring supplies in to the major coastal

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:48.980
stations, and from there the internal logistics
network gets to work.

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:53.300
Large planes are used to get as much cargo
and as many passengers to the continent as

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:55.139
inexpensively as possible.

00:05:55.139 --> 00:05:59.580
There are certain airports on other continents
that serve as gateways to the Antarctic.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:05.400
Christchurch, New Zealand Airport, for example,
sends about 100 flights per year and 5,500

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:10.530
passengers to Antarctica and serves as the
staging area for the New Zealand, American,

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:13.430
and Italian Antarctic logistics operations.

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:17.550
From there, it’s only a five hour flight
to McMurdo Station—the largest Antarctic

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:18.770
research base.

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:23.100
While Christchurch is the major Antarctic
gateway, flight do also leave from Cape Town,

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:25.440
South Africa and Punta Arenas, Chile.

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:30.139
These larger intercontinental planes typically
land at the major blue-ice and compacted snow

00:06:30.139 --> 00:06:35.300
runways near the coast, but then many of these
passengers and much of this cargo needs to

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:36.600
get inland.

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:40.970
The inland research bases tend to be smaller
and there are fewer of them, but they are

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:42.580
still significant.

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:48.270
The American Amundsen–Scott South Pole Station,
for example, has a population of 150 people

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:54.090
in the summer and there are also smaller Italian,
French, Russian, Japanese, and German stations

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:55.430
away from the coasts.

00:06:55.430 --> 00:07:00.400
For the American Antarctic operations, McMurdo
station operates as the logistics hub.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:05.639
Nearly all cargo and passengers arrive there
on larger cargo planes or cargo ships.

00:07:05.639 --> 00:07:10.889
From there, passengers and some cargo are
transferred most often onto Lockheed LC-130

00:07:10.889 --> 00:07:12.000
planes.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:16.670
These prop planes are specifically designed
for Arctic and Antarctic operations.

00:07:16.670 --> 00:07:21.310
They have retractable skis that allow them
to land on soft, non-compacted snow and there

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:23.510
are only ten in existence.

00:07:23.510 --> 00:07:27.930
Polar operations often mean taking off at
high altitudes where the air in thin.

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:32.260
The Amundsen–Scott South Pole Station, where
the plane often flies to, for example, is

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:35.620
surprisingly at 9,300 feet above sea-level.

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:40.060
That’s even higher than the highest elevation
commercial airport in the US.

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:45.290
When the air is thin wings generate less lift
so the speed needed to takeoff is higher and

00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:51.620
so, in order to be able to takeoff at higher
elevations, this LC-130 plane has rockets

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:53.540
to help speed it up at take-off.

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:58.250
Thanks to its skis, this plane can operate
to those places like the South Pole station

00:07:58.250 --> 00:08:01.170
that don’t have compacted snow or blue ice
runways.

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:06.440
While passengers and some cargo like fresh
food take the quick two hour flight from McMurdo

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:09.510
Station to the South Pole, there is another
way.

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:14.560
Flights are hugely expensive and the United
States Antarctic Program works on a limited

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:18.210
budget so there’s an effort being made to
reduce shipping costs.

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:20.750
Therefore, they built a road.

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:26.580
Just like the runways this road is made from
compacted snow and stretches 995 miles from

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:28.890
McMurdo Station to the South Pole.

00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:33.610
Using this South Pole Traverse, the United
States Antarctic Program runs convoys of tractors

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:37.150
pulling sleds of cargo across the ice and
snow.

00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:42.610
This trip takes about 40 days one-way, but
it still is significantly cheaper than flights

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:47.330
and can handle cargo too large to fit in an
LC-130 cargo plane.

00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:52.649
Of course, Antarctica is still Antarctica—one
of the harshest climates in the world.

00:08:52.649 --> 00:08:57.640
Whenever a plane leaves from New Zealand or
South Africa or Chile to Antarctica, it’s

00:08:57.640 --> 00:09:02.740
required to take enough fuel to fly all the
way to its destination, attempt landing, then

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:05.760
fly back to its origin if landing is not possible.

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:10.631
Planes fail, equipment breaks, and weather
changes, so Antarctica just isn’t a place

00:09:10.631 --> 00:09:12.730
conducive to reliability.

00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:17.500
For this reason, planes are prohibited from
landing or taking off in the dark and of course,

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:22.210
in the winter in Antarctica, it’s dark for
24 hours a day.

00:09:22.210 --> 00:09:28.220
Therefore, for seven months out of the year,
there are no planes, no boats, no link at

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:31.779
all between Antarctica and the rest of the
world.

00:09:31.779 --> 00:09:36.270
The lack of transport links during the winter
have as much to do with the cold as the dark.

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:41.230
At McMurdo station where most ships dock on
the coast, the winter temperature rarely rises

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:46.050
above zero degrees Fahrenheit meaning the
coast is blocked with sea-ice and meanwhile

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:52.750
at the South Pole station, the average July
high temperature is -67 degrees Fahrenheit

00:09:52.750 --> 00:09:57.680
meaning that if any plane landed there, its
fuel would freeze within minutes.

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:03.020
Of course, the large bases, like McMurdo Station
which balloons to well over 1,000 residents

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:07.330
in the summer, need maintenance over the winter
and some science experiments need to be conducted

00:10:07.330 --> 00:10:13.709
year round so people have to stay in Antarctica,
alone, in the dark, for the entire winter

00:10:13.709 --> 00:10:16.380
with no link to the outside world.

00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:21.410
In recent years there have been a small number
of exceptions to this lack of flights in winter,

00:10:21.410 --> 00:10:25.570
mostly due to medical evacuation flights,
but for the most part, once the last plane

00:10:25.570 --> 00:10:30.790
leaves in February, everyone still in Antarctica
is stuck there until the following November.

00:10:30.790 --> 00:10:37.010
All food, fuel, and supplies are stocked there
well before and a small number of people—45

00:10:37.010 --> 00:10:41.600
in the case of the south pole station—stick
around to keep the bases running.

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:45.900
In a sense, these people who stay the winter
in Antarctica are even more isolated than

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:48.950
the astronauts on the International Space
Station.

00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:54.131
There are few places humans can go where they
are seven months away from medical care, from

00:10:54.131 --> 00:10:56.800
food, from civilization.

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:01.580
Those living and working on the last continent
endure some of the harshest conditions on

00:11:01.580 --> 00:11:07.000
this earth, but for the pursuit of science,
all this hardship, all this work, and all

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:10.399
this cost is worth it.

00:11:10.399 --> 00:11:14.080
If you want to live and work in Antarctica,
your best shot to get there is if you’re

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:15.080
a scientist.

00:11:15.080 --> 00:11:19.290
In particular, a lot of those working there
are astronomers and the best place to get

00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:23.089
a basic understanding of astronomy is brilliant.org.

00:11:23.089 --> 00:11:27.460
Brilliant’s interactive quizzes teach you
